    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe chain rule can be used to differentiate exponential functions like\n[MATH: y = e^{3x}.]\n\nContent:\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n[MATH: y = e^u, \\qquad u = 3x.]\n\nContent:\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (d)/(du)(e^{u}) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(3x); = e^{u} \u22c5 3; = 3e^{u}]\n\nContent:\nSubstituting [MATH: u=3x,] we obtain the final result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= 3e^{3x}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: f'(x)] if [MATH: f(x) = e^{5x +4}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we express [MATH: f(x)] as the composite function\n[MATH: f = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n[MATH: (df)/(dx)|= (df)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = e^{u} \u22c5 5; = 5e^{u}; = 5e^{5x + 4}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: f(x) = e^{2x + 5},] find [MATH: (df)/(dx).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2xe^{2x + 5}]\nb. [MATH: e^{2x + 5}]\nc. [MATH: (e^{2x + 5})/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (2x + 5)e^{2x + 5}]\ne. [MATH: 2e^{2x + 5}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: f(x) = e^{3x},] find [MATH: (df)/(dx).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: xe^{3x}]\nb. [MATH: 3xe^{3x}]\nc. [MATH: (e^{3x})/(3)]\nd. [MATH: 3e^{3x}]\ne. [MATH: e^{3x}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nGiven that [MATH: y=e^{-x^2},] calculate [MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst we express [MATH: y] as the composite function\n\n[MATH: y = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = e^{u} \u22c5 (\u2212 2x); = \u2212 2xe^{u}; = \u2212 2xe^{\u2212 x^{2}}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: y = e^{x^{4} + 1},] find [MATH: (dy)/(dx).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4x^{3}e^{x^{4} + 1}]\nb. [MATH: 4x^{3} + e^{x^{4} + 1}]\nc. [MATH: 4x^{4}e^{x^{4} + 1}]\nd. [MATH: 4x^{5}e^{x^{4} + 1}]\ne. [MATH: 4x^{5} + e^{x^{4} + 1}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: f^{\u2032}(x)] for [MATH: f(x) = e^{x^{2}}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: xe^{x^{2}}]\nb. [MATH: e^{2x^{2}}]\nc. [MATH: 2e^{x^{2}}]\nd. [MATH: x^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 2xe^{x^{2}}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nGiven that [MATH: y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},] find [MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we express [MATH: y] as the composite function\n\n[MATH: y = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = 3 \u22c5 2^{u}\\ln (2) \u22c5 (\u2212 1); = \u2212 3 \u22c5 2^{u}\\ln (2); = \u2212 3 \u22c5 2^{1 \u2212 x}\\ln (2).]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: y = 3^{6x^{2} \u2212 4},] find [MATH: (dy)/(dx).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (12x)3^{6x^{2} \u2212 4}\\ln (3)]\nb. [MATH: (12x)3^{6x^{2} \u2212 4}]\nc. [MATH: (12x \u2212 4)3^{6x^{2} \u2212 4}\\ln (3)]\nd. [MATH: (12x^{3})3^{12x}\\ln (3)]\ne. [MATH: (12x \u2212 4)3^{6x^{2} \u2212 4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: f(x) = 3^{2x^{2}},] find [MATH: f^{\u2032}(x).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x3^{2x}\\ln (3)]\nb. [MATH: 2x3^{2x^{2}}]\nc. [MATH: 2 \u22c5 3^{2x}\\ln (3)]\nd. [MATH: 4x3^{2x^{2}}\\ln (3)]\ne. [MATH: 4x3^{2x^{2}}]"
  }
